     },
     "type": "TextDisplay",
     "title": "How to read this",
     "config": {
      "locationUrl": "",
      "locationFile": "",
      "editorData": "<div style=\"font-family:'Metropolis','Segoe UI',sans-serif;font-size:13px;padding:12px 14px;background:#2b3137;color:#e9edf0;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);border-radius:6px\">\n<h2 style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;margin:2px 0 10px 0;color:#e9edf0\">Host resources &mdash; is vSAN slow, or is the host out of road?</h2>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">Every latency figure on every other screen is only meaningful if the host had capacity to spare. This screen answers that first, so the others can be trusted. It shows what <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">vSAN itself</b> consumes on a host &mdash; memory, CPU, network &mdash; not the host's total load, which is vCenter's to report and appears on the Overview and Network screens.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">CPU contention matters more than CPU usage, and <code style=\"font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:12px;color:#8fd3ff\">readyPct</code> is the headline.</b> A host can be busy with nothing waiting, and can make things wait while looking only moderately busy. Ready time is the one that hurts: it is vSAN's threads queued for a core they did not get. Used and run percentages are context for it, not peers of it.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">This table is per host, four rows, not per core.</b> It replaced a per-CPU table of 244 rows that carried only generic utilisation and no ready time &mdash; more rows, less answer. If you need to find a single hot core, that is a vCenter question, not a vSAN one.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">Start with service memory.</b> A daemon that is not answering explains more than any latency number will. This pack can measure what clomd, cmmdsd and the rest consume and reserve; it cannot see whether the process is responding, so a flat line here is not the same as a healthy daemon.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">The network table is vSAN's traffic only, aggregated per host.</b> It is the same counters the Network screen breaks out per vmknic, summed &mdash; useful here as a resource figure (what is this host moving) rather than a fault-finding one. If a number looks wrong, the Network screen has the layer detail behind it.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">What this screen cannot tell you.</b> Nothing here reports host memory pressure or swap &mdash; those are the host's totals, not vSAN's share, and they come from vCenter. A host can be swapping hard while every figure on this screen looks calm.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">Reading any column here</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">A blank cell is not a zero.</b> Where a driver refuses a read, or a rate has no previous sample, this pack emits nothing rather than a zero &mdash; because a zero would claim the thing was measured and was fine.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">Rate columns are the maximum over the interval, counters marked Raw are cumulative since boot.</b> They are not two views of one number: a Raw column beside its rate is a history beside a present reading, and neither is a duplicate of the other.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">vSAN daemon memory</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">Renders the <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">vSAN Host Memory</b> view.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Kernel consumed size</span> &mdash; Memory the vSAN kernel modules are actually using. <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">Measured at 2.14 GB against 21.0 GB reserved</b>, so roughly a tenth of the reservation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Kernel Reserved Memory Size</span> &mdash; Memory reserved for the vSAN kernel modules. Reserved is not consumed -- compare the two columns rather than reading either alone.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Uw consumed size</span> &mdash; Memory consumed by vSAN user-world processes in total.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">User world Reserved Memory Size</span> &mdash; Memory reserved for vSAN user-world processes.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Clomd consumed size</span> &mdash; Memory used by <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">clomd</b>, which decides where objects and their replicas are placed. Growth here tracks object count, not IO.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Clomd reserved size</span> &mdash; Memory reserved for clomd.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Cmmdsd consumed size</span> &mdash; Memory used by <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">cmmdsd</b>, the cluster directory service that maintains membership and object metadata.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Cmmdsd reserved size</span> &mdash; Memory reserved for cmmdsd.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Vsanmgmtd consumed size</span> &mdash; Memory used by the vSAN management daemon, which answers the API calls this pack itself makes.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Epd consumed size</span> &mdash; Memory used by <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">epd</b>, which handles object distribution.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Osfsd consumed size</span> &mdash; Memory used by <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">osfsd</b>, the object filesystem daemon that presents vSAN objects as namespaces.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Vsandevicemonitord consumed size</span> &mdash; Memory used by the device monitor, which watches disks for failure and degradation.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">vSAN CPU - is it starved?</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">Renders the <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">vSAN Host CPU</b> view.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Used % (max)</span> &mdash; Peak physical CPU consumed by vSAN's threads in the interval. <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">This and Max Run are the only two CPU figures vSAN actually populates</b>; the plain and average variants return zero in every sample. On an otherwise light cluster it sits at 3 and peaks at 7, which fits a share of the WHOLE HOST under vSAN's thread cap rather than a share of vSAN's own slice -- but that denominator is <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">inferred, not confirmed</b>. A load test driving it past 100 would settle it.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Run % (max)</span> &mdash; The highest run percentage observed in the interval, across vSAN's threads. One of only two CPU figures vSAN populates. Read it beside Max Used: a gap between them points at scheduling overhead rather than work.</p>\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;font-weight:700;margin:16px 0 6px 0;color:#e9edf0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);padding-bottom:3px\">Host network - all vSAN</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\">Renders the <b style=\"font-weight:700;color:#e9edf0\">vSAN Host Network</b> view.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Network In Packet Discard Rate</span> &mdash; Proportion of inbound packets discarded at the host network layer, across all of this host's vSAN traffic rather than one uplink.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Network Out Packet Discard Rate</span> &mdash; Proportion of outbound packets discarded at the host network layer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">In Packet Drop Rate (host port)</span> &mdash; The vSwitch discarded inbound packets for this port. A small, constant rate is usually normal: broadcast and multicast traffic the switch has nowhere to deliver. A rate that <i style=\"font-style:italic;color:#e9edf0\">changes</i> is the signal, not its presence.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Out Packet Drop Rate (host port)</span> &mdash; The vSwitch discarded outbound packets for this port.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Network In Throughput</span> &mdash; Inbound throughput for this host's vSAN traffic. Context for the error columns.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Network Out Throughput</span> &mdash; Outbound throughput for this host's vSAN traffic.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Network In Packets Per Second</span> &mdash; Inbound packets per second.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#e9edf0\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#8fd3ff\">Network Out Packets Per Second</span> &mdash; Outbound packets per second.</p>\n</div>\n",
